WEBVTT

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.540
I think I won't have to look at your ugly faces till we meet in the train next week.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.550
Three sailors on a seven-day leave.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.280
What can happen to them?

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.340
Well, one has a girl, so anything can happen.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.680
Another has a doting mother,

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.710
so aside from gaining a lot of weight,

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.060
almost nothing can happen.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.160
The third has seven days and nights,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.600
a welcome home party's planned,

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.990
so who knows what can happen.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:30.000
But besides the logical and normal experience,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.500
expectations of sailors on leave.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.290
Each of these men will have a moment utterly beyond the normal

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:42.160
and utterly outside all logic.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.070
A moment when all the rules of existence will no longer apply.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:51.950
Sheila?

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:52.600
Hmm?

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.490
Well, I was thinking, um, let's not wait.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:57.630
Let's get married this leave, eh?

00:01:58.510 --> 00:01:59.470
We can't, Johnny.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:03.200
- You've got to go and meet your people. - Well, I could get a special license.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.900
See, suppose I got sent abroad or something like that?

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.290
Johnny, you didn't say anything.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:13.800
Well, I mean, you know, we're going to rejoin the fleet soon. Let's go off a flow. We finished our refit.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:19.760
Thing is, I've only got four more days and...

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.730
That's all it is, Johnny.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.530
It's long enough for us to get engaged, but married, that's different.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.920
Something you want to plan and look forward to and...

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.320
Yeah. I suppose four days isn't very long, really, is it?

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:49.100
We'll not count the days, Johnny Watson. I'll go and make some tea.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.680
Yeah. Yeah, then we can get up before your mum and dad come back.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.340
Here, you got any of that shortbread left?

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.310
I like that.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:25.030
...fighting around to Brooke and Thunance, HMS Hurt was sunk by enemy action in the early

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:28.230
hours of the...

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:32.370
They've sunk the hood.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:33.390
What if it's just on the wireless?

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:34.390
Well, that's impossible.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:38.550
I said it went down straight away.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.370
Fifteen hundred men drowned.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.900
That's almost the entire ship's company.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.190
I can't understand why they didn't tell me to report back for duty.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:49.180
It doesn't make sense.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.320
Fifteen hundred men.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.280
What else did it say?

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:53.650
Nothing.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:54.890
Only that.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.890
But, Johnny, the hood's at Rosythe, isn't she?

00:03:56.890 --> 00:04:00.000
If she'd been bombed, we'd have had an alert here.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.920
here. It's only ten miles away. Unless she put to sea. They've only just come out a

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:11.620
dried dock. They couldn't have refuelled her in that time. Johnny, are you sure it was

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.740
the hood? Of course I'm sure. How can I make a mistake about a thing like that? The hood.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.630
That's what he said, the hood. Early this morning. Johnny, it would have happened this

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.430
morning. They wouldn't have announced it without informing the next of kin. They never

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:30.000
do. Look, I've just heard it on the wireless. What are you going to do? I'm going to find out

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.780
what's happened. I'll call the BBC. My Uncle William's an engineer there. We'll soon settle

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:54.960
this. I understand why they didn't call me. It's a question of any trouble, Sheila. But

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.060
it's not on the typescript of the news summary. How could it possibly be in the recording?

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:06.460
You know, Sheila, I've already violated every security regulation in the book by just letting you into the building.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:11.090
Do you want your old uncle to end up on the chopping block in the Tower of London?

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.450
Please, Uncle Bill, what harm could there be in it?

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.400
None. Come along.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:26.980
Here is the news, and this is Jeremy Smith reading it.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.940
Unique reports heavy fighting around Tobruk and Saloom.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.960
This is it.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.030
Aircraft of Bomber Command raided Bremen last night.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:42.530
Fires were started and harbour installations destroyed.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:50.340
All of them reports heavy fighting around Tobruk and Saloom.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.310
Aircraft of Bomber Command raided Bremen last night.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.470
Fires were started and harbour installations destroyed.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:00.000
What's that?

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:01.690
Right after he coughed.

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:09.680
Well.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:14.180
Thank you.

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.550
Fear of death can be a very important thing.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.320
The mind under stress, that's understandable.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:22.980
Do you think so?

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.320
Johnny, let's go.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:34.140
It was there.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:44.090
I thought I'd pop down to the air raid warden's post,

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:45.890
have a chat with Dad, perhaps a game of poker.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:07:00.000
Is it lonely for you?

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:11.470
in it, Ma. Dad working nights and all that. Get used to it. His neighbors and... You coming

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:18.740
out? Got that to look forward to. George, the time's gone so quick. You've got three

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:30.000
days, yeah? Ma, cheer up. It's not like you to be like this. I was being

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:45.150
is mine. That's what I want to see. George, you're not keeping anything from me, are you?

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:52.430
What do you mean? About where you're going after this, your ship. Look, I've told you,

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:59.630
Scarpa, Flo. Oh, look, come off it, Ma. We'll worry about it when the time comes, eh?

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.990
I mean, it's not as if I were on submarines or corvettes or something, is it?

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:11.260
Well, Dad's the one you ought to worry about.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:15.380
It's a lot safer in Scarpa Flow than here with all these bombs and things.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:17.320
Oh, come on.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.140
I'll be back in an hour, eh?

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:24.440
Let's see it.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:25.140
Let's see it.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.980
George, your hat's on.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.310
- Crooked. - Right?

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.380
Dad, there's gonna be a poker game tonight.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:11.790
Thought you might stay home with your mother, just this one evening.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:15.110
Dad, how is it with Mum, eh?

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:16.630
I don't know.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:19.210
It's only since she's been back this time.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:23.200
Oh, she's been through a lot, bombing and all.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.870
I'll have to sleep in there.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.790
The one thing she lives for is you coming home, don't we?

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.280
This time, I think it's all been a bit too much for her.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.960
No sooner, she gets used to having you around.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:35.280
Yeah, I know.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:38.260
I stay in this evening, huh?

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:40.580
See you in the morning.

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:41.830
Righto, George.

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:29.220
What's the matter?

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:43.850
Oh, George. George, you mustn't go. Not where they mean to send you.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:45.520
What are you talking about?

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.550
I dream of it, George. Every night since you got home.

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:57.130
Just now I dropped off. It was all so clear.

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:59.120
Now, don't say anything.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:12.520
I've got to tell you, George, it's a warning, George.

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:15.780
You can't ignore a thing like that.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:18.000
It must be sent for a reason.

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:23.990
All the time your dad was out in the Blitz during the First War,

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.050
I always knew it'd be all right.

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:27.690
I've always known.

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:30.000
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:48.690
about? Well, knocking at the front door. Boy with the telegram. One of them telegrams,

00:11:48.750 --> 00:12:00.000
you know. It's the same. Your son. Your son. Missing. Believed killed.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:11.430
In H.M. Minnesota, the date, May 24th.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:15.310
You will see the date, it said.

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:26.060
For pity's sake, Ma, you've rejoined my ship until the 22nd.

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:28.900
George, you've got to eat a warden like that.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:32.360
You know what your trouble is, don't you?

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:34.230
You're bomb happy.

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:37.550
You spend so much time in shelters,

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:41.750
wondering if you've got a home or a husband or any friends left.

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:47.030
You're like everyone else in Britain, on the brain.

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:56.440
You've been keeping this to yourself all this time,

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:57.820
spoiling my leave, haven't you?

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:00.000
Well, we're going to make up for that.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:07.210
We're going out, you and me, to every pub in the district, and let's have no more of this nonsense, eh?

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:25.460
So, take off your cardigan, put on your Sunday dress, do your hair, and ask me best girl.

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:30.000
- Ah, Peter. - But that's ridiculous. I haven't thought about Peter for years.

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:31.170
Really?

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:32.170
You haven't asked anything yet, Robin.

00:13:32.170 --> 00:13:33.170
You'll pay.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:34.170
No, of course not.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.580
Um, when's my birthday?

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:37.430
What month?

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:39.700
Jay?

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:45.190
Ten.

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:46.190
January.

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:59.370
Not bad, quite correct.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:00.000
January 4th.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.650
is my birthday. You're in touch. You're moving. Why not? It's electricity. More like in the

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:10.750
subconscious. Well, let's ask you something else. Yes, go on, Robin. All right, let's

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:22.100
think. I know about your ship. Yes, all right. Will I be going into action soon? No. In

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:28.560
three months' time, then. No. No action at all. Six months.

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:40.920
yeah you're gonna have a nice easy walk sitting up in the orkneys with all those cows you know

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:46.170
the system when you start talking to the cows the navy gives you a week's lead when the cows

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:57.200
start talking to you they send you home does mean i'm gonna live to a ripe old age yes let's ask it

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.440
and don't push it

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:34.650
Hang on, mate!

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:36.400
Look, that's my paper you've got there.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:37.450
Hey, Jack.

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:39.000
That's my paper, do you mind?

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.850
Buy your own paper if you want.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:43.730
Mate, give me my paper back, will ya?

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:45.360
Hey, look.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:48.050
So much later.

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:25.790
Hello, Johnny boy.

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:28.190
I've been looking for you, wondering if you're on the train.

00:16:28.870 --> 00:16:30.000
Robin's in the boppy. Come on, let's go.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:36.140
What's the matter?

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:48.060
George, do you believe that people can see into the future?

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:53.620
What do you mean?

00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:57.050
Well, what does that say?

00:16:57.050 --> 00:16:58.750
Big battle for Tobruk.

00:16:58.750 --> 00:16:59.760
Oh.

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:14.850
Now suppose, suppose you looked at that headline, suddenly, quite unexpectedly, and it said, now you knew it said, Hood Sunk.

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:16.300
All feared lost.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:17.210
Oh, wait a minute.

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:21.370
Now, what if this didn't happen to you once?

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:22.610
Twice.

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:30.000
Georgie, we've been mates a long time, you know.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:34.120
Something's going to happen to the hood, Georgie.

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:37.340
It's going to go down.

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:39.160
Something's going to happen on shore.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:44.920
You need a good strong cup of tea.

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:46.820
Come on, let's join Robin.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:04.470
Yeah, but don't you see, that's the point.

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:08.020
You see, it wouldn't matter if it only happened to me, but it's him as well.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:09.680
I tell you, it doesn't mean nothing.

00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:12.540
It was only a dream she had. I wish I hadn't mentioned it to you.

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:13.460
Well, what's the odds?

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:16.600
Anyway, I'm absolutely certain nothing whatever's going to happen to the hood.

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:20.500
You know, I've been taking a little look into the future myself.

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:22.720
You know, the old wine glass and all the letters and all that.

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:24.930
I'll tell you what it told me.

00:18:25.220 --> 00:18:30.000
I won't see any action for at least a year, and I'm going to live to a ripe old age.

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:32.700
It's my forecast, and I've no reason to doubt it.

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:35.230
Now, if nothing whatever's going to happen to me,

00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:36.910
how the heck can anything happen to you two?

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:39.220
Quite heaven, lad.

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:41.670
Quite heaven brings good luck.

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:45.110
What makes you think we're superstitious?

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:47.700
There's magic in the heather, real magic.

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:51.790
I gathered it myself, as it should be gathered,

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:54.880
with the saying of old spells,

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:57.480
as old as the Glen this came from.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:00.000
Can you tell fortunes?

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:07.870
Show me a man's hand. I'll read it like a book.

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:11.120
All right. Read that. Tell me what you see.

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:17.190
Nothing but happiness, sir. Happiness and contentment.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:21.840
No danger. I mean in the immediate future.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:28.210
Not a bit, sir. Nothing but happiness and good fortune.

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:34.370
You'll die in your bed at 95 with your grandchildren all around you.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.910
Oh, I see. Now you have a go.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:38.230
Oh, no, not me.

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:39.510
Oh, go on, don't be daft.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:49.060
There we are.

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:03.160
Stop thinking about it.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:04.700
It's going to be all right.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:05.380
Don't worry.

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:09.330
Yeah, heard the latest.

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:10.990
We got two hours notice to sail.

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:12.250
Us and the Prince of Wales.

00:20:12.650 --> 00:20:13.050
What?

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:14.950
I tell you, there's a regular flap on it.

00:20:14.990 --> 00:20:15.830
Something pretty big.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:21.690
Yeah, that means we'll be at sea on the 24th.

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:24.830
The date my mum saw in her dream.

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:26.560
Yes, we will.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:28.260
In an empty sea.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:29.470
Now shut up about it.

00:20:29.630 --> 00:20:30.000
Nothing's going to happen.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.800
happen to me so nothing's going to happen to you let's go to board singleman hughes come on get

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:42.400
moving hughes there's a movement order you've been posted for officer training portsmouth now

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:48.950
take your kid over to the transport office and they'll give you a railway warrant come on you two

00:21:03.360 --> 00:21:04.460
That's one for the book, eh?

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:23.150
Sure, Johnny.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:27.690
See you then, eh?

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:18.780
On May 24th, 1941, in the gray early morning,

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:23.280
His Majesty's ship Hood, along with other units of the Royal Navy,

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:28.520
engaged the powerful German battleship Bismarck in the Denmark Strait.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:36.160
A shell from the Bismarck struck the hood in her after-magazine with its store of ammunition.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:40.700
One minute later, when the smoke had cleared, she had disappeared from the surface of the

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:46.120
sea, taking with her 1,500 sons of England.

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:52.300
There were only three survivors of this tragedy, or more properly, we should say there were

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:53.900
four.

00:22:53.900 --> 00:23:00.000
And here's one: Actor Robin Hughes, one-time signalman of HMS Hood, later Lieutenant

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:08.440
commander hughes destroyer captain the lucky young man who was ordered away from death i don't think

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:13.500
i really believe in luck john why don't either really but why don't you well maybe because i'm

00:23:13.500 --> 00:23:20.950
welsh the welsh traditionally are given to things psychic in all my life i've had experiences dreams

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:30.000
premonitions some good some bad but most of them come true for instance i was in a bookstore once many miles

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:36.450
I had a chilling sensation, and in that instant I knew he was dead.

00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:44.110
I found myself holding an old French diary, and it had fallen open at my mother's birthday, April the 4th.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:48.640
And there was a single entry in French, je suis verve.

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:50.390
Je suis verve.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:52.460
It means, I am a widow.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:53.770
Extraordinary.

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:59.730
But most of us, sooner or later, make a contact with the world that lies beyond our understanding, don't we?

00:24:00.890 --> 00:24:06.790
now robin hughes joins me in a salute to the hood and to the valiant men who perished with her
